# Deploying a Static Website on AWS EC2 — Beginner's Guide

## Project Overview

In this project I deploy a locally developed static website to a publicly accessible AWS EC2 instance. The workflow is:

```
Local Machine → GitHub → AWS EC2 Instance → Public Internet
```

---

## Prerequisites

- A static website (HTML, CSS, assets) on my local machine
- A GitHub account
- An AWS account (free tier is sufficient)
- Git installed locally
- A terminal with SSH support

---

## Step 1 — Push Website Files to GitHub

First, I create a GitHub repository and push all local website files to it so I can later pull them onto the server.

```bash
# Navigate to the project folder
cd ~/Documents/my-website

# Initialize a Git repository
git init

# Stage all files
git add .

# Commit the files
git commit -m "Added website files"

# Link to the remote GitHub repository
git remote add origin https://github.com/<username>/<repo-name>.git

# Push to the master branch
git push -u origin master
```

> **Note:** If the `main` branch is protected on GitHub, push to `master` instead, or configure branch protection rules accordingly.

---

## Step 2 — Launch an AWS EC2 Instance

### 2.1 — Select a Region

- Log into the AWS Console at [aws.amazon.com](https://aws.amazon.com)
- Select a region close to my target audience (e.g., **Asia Pacific — Mumbai** for Indian users) to minimize latency

### 2.2 — Choose an AMI (OS Image)

- Navigate to **EC2 → Instances → Launch Instance**
- Select **Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL)** — free tier eligible
- Any free-tier eligible AMI works (Amazon Linux, Ubuntu, Debian, etc.)

### 2.3 — Choose an Instance Type

- Select **t2.micro** (1 vCPU, 1 GB RAM) — free tier eligible
- AWS supports scaling up to 128 vCPUs / 256 GB RAM if needed in production

### 2.4 — Create a Key Pair

- Click **Create new key pair**
- Give it a name (e.g., `demo-website`)
- Select **`.pem`** format (for OpenSSH on Linux/Mac) or **`.ppk`** (for PuTTY on Windows)
- Download and store the `.pem` file securely — **it cannot be re-downloaded**
- The same key pair can be reused across multiple EC2 instances

### 2.5 — Configure Network Settings

| Setting | Value | Reason |
|---------|-------|--------|
| VPC | Default | Logical isolation of cloud resources |
| Auto-assign Public IP | **Enabled** | Required for public internet access |
| Allow SSH traffic | My IP (recommended) or Anywhere | Login access |
| Allow HTTP traffic | **Enabled** | Required to serve the website |

> **Security tip:** Restricting SSH to **My IP** means even if the private key is leaked, an attacker cannot SSH in from a different IP address.

> **HTTP vs HTTPS:** For this demo, HTTP is used. In production, redirect HTTP → HTTPS at the load balancer level.

### 2.6 — Configure Storage

- Set storage to **10 GB SSD** (EBS) — free tier allows up to 30 GB

### 2.7 — Launch the Instance

- Click **Launch Instance**
- The instance moves to **running** state within seconds
- Note the assigned **Public IPv4 address**

---

## Step 3 — Connect to the EC2 Instance via SSH

```bash
# Navigate to the folder containing the .pem key
cd ~/Downloads

# Restrict key file permissions (required by SSH)
chmod 400 demo-website.pem

# SSH into the EC2 instance
ssh -i "demo-website.pem" ec2-user@<public-ip-address>
```

- Default username for RHEL/Amazon Linux is `ec2-user`
- Type `yes` when prompted to confirm the host fingerprint

After connecting, the terminal prompt changes to show the EC2 instance hostname — I am now inside the remote server.

---

## Step 4 — Set Up the Server

### 4.1 — Switch to Root User

```bash
sudo su -
```

Regular users cannot install packages. `sudo su -` switches to the root user which has full system privileges.

### 4.2 — Update the System

```bash
yum update -y
```

The `-y` flag auto-confirms all prompts.

### 4.3 — Install Git

```bash
yum install git -y

# Verify installation
git --version
```

### 4.4 — Install Apache Web Server

```bash
yum install httpd -y
```

Apache (`httpd`) is the web server that listens for incoming HTTP requests and serves website files.

> Apache serves files located at: **`/var/www/html/`**  
> Any files placed in this directory will be publicly accessible.

### 4.5 — (Optional) Install Node.js for Dynamic Apps

```bash
yum install nodejs -y
```

---

## Step 5 — Deploy the Website

### 5.1 — Clone the GitHub Repository

```bash
# Go to home directory
cd ~

# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/<username>/<repo-name>.git

# Enter the cloned folder
cd <repo-name>

# If files are on the master branch, switch to it
git checkout master
```

### 5.2 — Copy Website Files to Apache's Serving Directory

```bash
# Copy the main HTML file
cp index.html /var/www/html/

# Copy folders/directories (requires -r for recursive copy)
cp -r assets/ /var/www/html/
cp -r errors/ /var/www/html/
cp -r images/ /var/www/html/
```

> Use `cp -r` for directories. Using `cp` without `-r` on a folder will throw an error: `omitting directory`.

### 5.3 — Verify Files are in Place

```bash
cd /var/www/html/
ls
pwd
# Expected output: /var/www/html
```

---

## Step 6 — Start the Apache Web Server

```bash
# Start the Apache service
systemctl start httpd

# Enable it to start automatically on reboot
systemctl enable httpd

# Verify it is running
systemctl status httpd
```

Now open a browser and navigate to:

```
http://<public-ip-address>
```

The website should be live and publicly accessible.

> **Browser tip:** If the page does not load, check that the browser is not automatically redirecting to `https://`. Manually type `http://` at the start of the URL — the server only listens on HTTP in this setup.

---

## Key Concepts Covered

### VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)
- A logically isolated network within AWS where my resources live
- Controls who can access my instances at the network level
- Different from VPN — VPN creates a secure tunnel for data in transit; VPC isolates the cloud environment itself

### Security Groups (Firewall)
- Act as a virtual firewall controlling inbound and outbound traffic
- Rules I configured:
- **Port 22 (SSH):** Allow login access
- **Port 80 (HTTP):** Allow public web traffic

### Key Pair Authentication
- AWS uses key pair (public/private key) instead of passwords for SSH
- The `.pem` file is the private key — keep it safe and never share it
- `chmod 400` restricts the file to read-only by the owner, which SSH requires

### IAM (Identity and Access Management)
- Controls which users or roles can access which AWS resources
- Follows the **principle of least privilege** — only grant the minimum permissions needed
- Example: a developer can be given read-only access to one specific EC2 instance, not the entire AWS account

### Public IP vs Private IP
- **Public IP:** Accessible from the internet — used for web servers, SSH access
- **Private IP:** Only accessible within the VPC — used for internal resources like databases that should never be exposed publicly

### Apache Web Server (`httpd`)
- Listens on port 80 for incoming HTTP requests
- Serves any files placed in `/var/www/html/`
- Must be explicitly started with `systemctl start httpd`

---

## Troubleshooting

| Problem | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| `Permission denied` when running `yum` | Not root user | Run `sudo su -` first |
| `cp` throws "omitting directory" error | Missing `-r` flag | Use `cp -r <folder> <destination>` |
| Website not loading in browser | Apache not started | Run `systemctl start httpd` |
| Browser redirects to `https://` | Browser HTTPS upgrade | Manually type `http://` in the URL bar |
| SSH connection refused | SSH rule missing in Security Group | Add inbound rule for port 22 |
| `git: command not found` | Git not installed | Run `yum install git -y` |

---

## Next Steps

- Replace the static site with a **Node.js** or **Python** dynamic application
- Point a **custom domain** to the EC2 public IP using Route 53 or any DNS provider
- Add **HTTPS** using AWS Certificate Manager + a load balancer, or use Let's Encrypt
- Use **Dockerize** the application and run it as a container
- Explore **Auto Scaling Groups** to handle traffic spikes automatically
- Move static files to **S3 + CloudFront** for a fully managed, scalable static hosting solution
